---
name: ptt-mktask
description: "As a designer, draft a task from the agreed discussion, release a draft for taking, or amend a released charter. Arguments: none (draft), release N|name, amend N."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Make a task

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes; other work needs AtE (rules file). Only a designer makes tasks: the
holder of `T0000` the top-level tasks, the holder of a `design` task the
subtasks of its scope. `<name>` is your holder name.

## Draft (no argument)

1. From the recent discussion, decide with the user: a slug
   (`wall-distance`), the kind (`track` research, `worker` implementation,
   `design` a sub-scope with its own tasks), and the tasks it depends on, if
   any; also a tool restriction if needed (`claude`, `codex`, or `any`,
   the default when absent).
2. `<records>/bin/ptt mktask <slug> --as <name> --kind <kind> [--depends N,N] [--tool claude|codex|any]`
   prints the charter's path.
3. Fill in the charter from the discussion (or a brief the user names, cited
   as its source): goal, read-first list by repository, path, and commit or
   request number, scope, where results go, merge order, out of scope,
   acceptance criteria checkable from records. Include at least one check
   of the goal itself, as a command where one exists, with its expected
   result (rules file, goal acceptance). In `Merge order`, give each
   `after: <task or request>`, its reason and when it clears, or write
   "Independent of other open tasks." `depends:` gates taking until the
   dependencies are closed; merge order gates merging while work may start.
   Sub-designers do the same for their subtasks. Mark what the user agreed
   and what you propose.
4. Show it to the user. A draft stays editable until released; record edits
   with `<records>/bin/ptt record -m "task <N>: charter draft" <charter>`.

## Release (`release N`)

For a complete draft: `<records>/bin/ptt ready <N> --as <name>`: the charter
is committed and frozen; the task can be taken (`/ptt-take <N>`). Pass on
the printed lines that start a new session taking it (only the permitted
tool for a restricted task),
for the user to paste in the workspace root.

## Amend (`amend N`)

A released charter is never edited; an agreed change is an amendment:
`<records>/bin/ptt amend <N> --as <name> --text "<change>"`, with `--depends
N,N` to replace its dependencies (checked for cycles), and `--tool
claude|codex|any` to replace its tool restriction. This affects future takes;
an existing holder keeps the task. A holder sees it in
its inbox (with an active prompt hook, at the user's next message;
otherwise at its next `/ptt-inbox`).

A change of merge order amends the waiting task: its `after:` decides;
`before:` in the other charter is only a pointer. To put a new task ahead
of an earlier one, amend that earlier, waiting task. The amendment reaches
its holder through the inbox; no extra note to the holder is needed. If
the waiting task already has a request submitted, also record a note to
the integrator (`to: integrator`) naming that request and the amendment.
A merged request cannot be reordered.
